    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d189">

                <pb facs="0376" n="248" sameAs="frus:frus1885/pg248"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">No. 189.</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Bayard to Mr.
                            Becerra</hi>.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1885-04-06"><hi rendition="#italic">April</hi> 6,
                        1885</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Referring to so much of our conversation of
                    the 3d instant as related to the dispatch of telegrams from you to the officers
                    of the Colombian federal Government at Panama and Colon, respectively, General
                    Gónima and Colonel Ulloa, I have the honor to communicate to you herewith copy
                    of a telegram which I have received from Mr. Scrymser, the president of the
                    Central and South American Telegraph Company, in which he reports for your
                    information that he has been unable to cause the delivery of those telegrams,
                    inasmuch as General Gónima is a prisoner in the hands of the Radicals who have
                    taken Panama, and Colonel Ulloa is lying seriously wounded at Colon.</p>
                <p>Accept, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>T. F. BAYARD.</signed></closer>


                <xi:include href="d189-a1.xml" xpointer="d189-a1"/>
            </div>
